  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(x_{k+1}=x_k+\\dfrac12\\) for \\(k=1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(\\ldots,\\) \\(n-1\\) and \\(x_1=1,\\) find \\(x_1+x_2+\\cdots+x_n.\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{n+3}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2-1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2+n}{4}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{n^2+3n}{4}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1962-p32.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We have \\(x_n=1+\\frac{n-1}{2}=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\\) Therefore \\[ \\begin{aligned} x_1+\\cdots+x_n &=\\frac n2\\left(1+\\frac{n+1}{2}\\right)\\\\ &=\\frac{n^2+3n}{4}. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "The recurrence defines an arithmetic sequence with common difference \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)",
        "Find \\(x_n\\), then use \\(\\frac{n(x_1+x_n)}{2}\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
